{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773343296,"nonce":"D8/+/6y2X5m9TuZbhU4DRkERSeM=","title":"Auth queue: add dependency field so approval order can be enforced across repos","message":"When submitting an auth request, allow specifying a dependency auth request ID. The dashboard would then present a warning modal when the user tries to approve a request whose dependency has not yet completed.\n\n## CLI\n```\nmandible auth request \u003cbranch\u003e --depends-on \u003crequest-id\u003e ...\n```\n\n## DB\nAdd `depends_on_id TEXT` column to `auth_requests` table (nullable).\n\n## Dashboard UX\nWhen the user clicks \"Approve\" on a request with an unmet dependency (`depends_on_id` set and dependency `exit_code != 0` or `status != completed`):\n- Show a modal overlaid within the card/display region only (not the sidebar, not the app header)\n- Three buttons:\n  1. **Approve anyway** — proceed with approval ignoring the dependency\n  2. **Go to dependency** — same effect as clicking the dependency entry in the sidebar: show its card, scroll to and highlight it in the sidebar\n  3. **Cancel** — dismiss modal, no other effect (same as clicking outside modal within card area)\n\n## Motivation\nOrdering failures like the dispatcher-in-repo / mandible-dispatcher-shim incident: dotfiles shim merged before mandible bin/mandible existed, breaking the CLI. A depends_on would have surfaced this in the dashboard before approval.","files":null}]}